# KaminIDE patches — gpui-pre-windows 0.3.8 (zed 279fe070bb389b79652e52065b2f001edcc0b11b)
#
# 1. Debug shaders: embed both HLSL sources and their shared include, compile with rooted virtual filenames and the existing flags, and keep dev/agent binaries independent of the build worktree. The scoped include callback borrows static bytes until synchronous D3DCompile returns; its raw out-pointers are supplied by the compiler. Release keeps its existing embedded bytecode.
# 2. Frame demand: wake idle Windows windows asynchronously through the existing GPUI invalidator, coalescing WM_PAINT and retaining paced vsync retry during native draws; weak window ownership prevents stale HWND requests.
# 3. Settled subtree skipping: ink sinks collect the placed bounds of every primitive a scope offers (culled included, deferred paint snapshots feed the sinks open at capture) and are tainted by input, element state, groups or callbacks unless a scope declares them inert while unseen; exact layout leaves stand in for inflexible column items whose descendant margins do not adjoin their edges, and are plain leaves of the retained frame tree.
# 4. Frame diagnostics: opt-in host logging exposes native draw force and pending force without changing requests.
# 5. Deferred frames: consume the pending forced request even when the incoming draw is already forced, preventing a stale extra presentation.
# 6. DirectX groups: one frame-domain staging surface preserves GPU interpolation exactly; scissored writes, dirty-rectangle clears, bounded pooled retained surfaces with blur margins, and rectangle-only blend backdrop copies.
# 7. Adapter policy: instance-owned typed DXGI preference, unchanged default enumeration, device recovery and live adapter LUID/driver identity.
# 8. DirectWrite: CSS font selection, shaping, vertical glyphs and fallback metrics.
# 9. DirectX: CSS primitives, images, filters, masks, gradients and per-window frame readback with copy/map duration delivered to the explicit host endpoint.
# 10. Window lifecycle: per-window rendering/resize policy and deterministic WPT scale.
# 11. Process shutdown: drain deferred native window destruction, stop and join owned vsync threads while pumping messages, then release GPU/text devices before leaving the platform loop.
# 12. Diagnostics: application-owned GPU batch measurements and typed startup configuration survive device recovery; environment parsing belongs to the host.
# 13. Tests: initialize unfragmented backdrop fixtures against the paired GPUI surface fields.
# 14. Unsafe invariants: added unsafe blocks are windows-rs COM/Win32 calls on interfaces owned by the live DirectX/DirectWrite state, called on the thread that owns that state, with HRESULTs and out-parameters checked before use. Raw memory outside embedded debug shader compilation is touched in three places only: the frame-capture readback copies w*4 bytes per row of a w x h BGRA8 staging texture between Map and Unmap (RowPitch >= w*4), GPU timing reads a query result through the raw GetData vtable entry into a plain-data out-value whose size is passed with it, and external-texture registration adopts a non-null ID3D11Texture2D pointer already AddRef'd by the caller (from_raw takes that reference). Release builds never select the debug-only WARP/D3D11On12 device overrides.
# 15. Offscreen windows: a platform option creates message-only windows whose renderer draws into a render-target texture of the same device, format and shaders instead of a swap chain, with no DirectComposition target, Present, DWM attributes or resize presentation boost; coalesced posted paint messages replace WM_PAINT for frame demand and vsync pacing, and device recovery rebuilds the offscreen target. Measurement and pixel-gate hosts read frames back without loading the desktop compositor.
# 16. Presentation never blocks the UI thread (BR-31A): Present uses DXGI_PRESENT_DO_NOT_WAIT; a frame dropped by it or by the frame-latency gate keeps the gate count it took and makes the next paint present the current scene; Present time and dropped frames feed the frame counters.
# 17. GPU memory report: the DirectX renderer names every texture it holds (atlas pages with their allocated share, frame and path targets, group and blur buffers) by owner path, on request only; the software rasterizer is an explicit adapter preference that no selector string parses to, so optimized benchmark builds can measure software rendering while application selection is unchanged.
# 18. Damage redraw: the target keeps the last frame and every buffer (offscreen texture or flip-model back buffer) carries the damage accumulated since it was drawn; a frame is drawn only within that rectangle under a scissor, groups whose composite cannot reach it are skipped, and an unchanged frame is neither drawn nor presented. A different size, background, opaque backdrop, atlas epoch (removed tiles, replaced or forgotten external textures, device loss), a sampled external texture, a failed pass or GPUI_DAMAGE_REDRAW=0 draw the whole frame. Frame capture keeps one staging texture per size. Group images stay in the group atlas between frames: a group matched with a group of the last frame (Scene::group_base) keeps that group's atlas rectangle and image and is not drawn again; the other groups take free space on the shelves of the last full layout, and the atlas is packed again when they do not fit.
